    {
      "severity": "moderate",
      "description": "Sarkhan, Dragon Ascendant is a 2/2 creature that can potentially transform into a powerful dragon, but the opponent also has Momo, Friendly Flier on the battlefield. Momo is a 1/1 flier that likely provides card advantage or other synergies. However, the key issue is that Burst Lightning deals 2 damage, which kills either Sarkhan or Charming Prince equally. Sarkhan as a 2/2 planeswalker-creature is a significant threat that can transform, so targeting it is defensible. But looking at the board more carefully - the player has Lightning Helix in hand which also deals 3 damage and could kill any of these creatures. The Burst Lightning could have been used on Momo (1/1) to save the more efficient removal for the bigger threat. Using Burst Lightning (2 damage) on a 2/2 when you could use it on the 1/1 Momo and save Lightning Helix for Sarkhan (or use it to go face for the life gain) represents suboptimal resource allocation. However, Sarkhan's dragon transformation potential makes it arguably the highest priority target. The real question is whether killing Momo with Burst Lightning and Sarkhan with Lightning Helix would have been better sequencing, since Lightning Helix gains 3 life and Burst Lightning is the cheaper/less valuable removal spell being used on a higher-value target.",
